26-09-11","what_it_is":"β-Bisabolene is a naturally occurring monocyclic sesquiterpene, a type of aromatic molecule that helps give certain plants their characteristic warm, woody, and subtly spicy scents. You likely encounter it in the kitchen whenever you slice fresh ginger, zest a lemon, or chop carrots. In the plant world, it acts as part of the botanical defense system, but in daily life, it contributes to the complex, comforting aromas of our favorite culinary spices and herbs.","found_in":"This compound is famously abundant in the essential oils of ginger (Zingiber officinale) and turmeric (Curcuma longa). It is also found in everyday foods like carrots and lemons, as well as aromatic herbs like valerian and cannabis.","why_researchers_care":"Researchers are drawn to β-bisabolene for its broad biological activity, particularly its potential to fight off microbes and protect cells from oxidative stress. Early laboratory work has shown it can neutralize certain bacteria and interact with key inflammatory pathways, prompting scientists to explore how it might support human health. The strongest evidence so far comes from human observational studies (Oxford level 2b), which are currently guiding deeper investigations into its role as a natural antioxidant and protective agent.","structure_function":[{"claim_id":"antimicrobial","statement":"Observational research suggests β-bisabolene may support the body's natural defenses against certain microbes.","evidence_level":"human_observational","basis":"This rests on 32 papers, including 11 human observational studies and numerous microbial sensitivity tests.","eco":{"id":"ECO:0000180","label":"clinical study evidence"},"oxford":{"level":"2b","label":"individual cohort or observational study"},"evidence_lines":[{"design":"human_observational","eco":"ECO:0000180","papers":11},{"design":"animal","eco":"ECO:0007120","papers":4},{"design":"in_vitro","eco":"ECO:0000181","papers":10},{"design":"review","eco":"ECO:0000033","papers":1}],"paper_count":32},{"claim_id":"antioxidant","statement":"Human observational studies indicate this molecule may help protect cells from oxidative stress.","evidence_level":"human_observational","basis":"This is supported by 20 papers exploring its antioxidant properties across various models.","eco":{"id":"ECO:0000180","label":"clinical study evidence"},"oxford":{"level":"2b","label":"individual cohort or observational study"},"evidence_lines":[{"design":"human_observational","eco":"ECO:0000180","papers":6},{"design":"animal","eco":"ECO:0007120","papers":4},{"design":"in_vitro","eco":"ECO:0000181","papers":1},{"design":"review","eco":"ECO:0000033","papers":1}],"paper_count":20},{"claim_id":"anti-inflammatory","statement":"Early observational work points toward a role in supporting healthy inflammatory responses.","evidence_level":"human_observational","basis":"This is based on 7 papers investigating its interaction with inflammatory pathways.","eco":{"id":"ECO:0000180","label":"clinical study evidence"},"oxford":{"level":"2b","label":"individual cohort or observational study"},"evidence_lines":[{"design":"human_observational","eco":"ECO:0000180","papers":3},{"design":"animal","eco":"ECO:0007120","papers":2},{"design":"review","eco":"ECO:0000033","papers":1}],"paper_count":7}],"hypothesized_benefits":[{"claim_id":"cancer","area":"Cellular Health","hypothesis":"Researchers are exploring whether β-bisabolene's observed cytotoxicity in breast cancer cell lines translates to protective effects in the human body. If this bears out, it could become a valuable natural compound in cellular health research.","evidence_level":"human_observational","paper_count":7,"study_to_test_it":"A randomized, double-blind, placebo-controlled clinical trial administering a standardized β-bisabolene extract to healthy adults, measuring specific biomarkers of cellular proliferation and oxidative stress over six months.","eco":{"id":"ECO:0000180","label":"clinical study evidence"},"oxford":{"level":"2b","label":"individual cohort or observational study"},"evidence_lines":[{"design":"human_observational","eco":"ECO:0000180","papers":7}]},{"claim_id":"neuroprotection","area":"Brain Health and Stroke Recovery","hypothesis":"Early animal work points toward a potential neuroprotective role, with researchers investigating whether it can ameliorate damage from ischemic stroke via specific antioxidant pathways. If confirmed, it could offer a new avenue for supporting brain recovery after injury.","evidence_level":"animal","paper_count":1,"study_to_test_it":"A first-in-human observational cohort study tracking dietary intake of β-bisabolene-rich foods in patients recovering from mild ischemic events, measuring cognitive and motor recovery scores over one year.","eco":{"id":"ECO:0007120","label":"animal model system study evidence"},"oxford":{"level":"5","label":"bench or animal research"},"evidence_lines":[{"design":"animal","eco":"ECO:0007120","papers":1}]},{"claim_id":"sleep","area":"Sleep and Relaxation","hypothesis":"Because it is found in calming herbs like valerian, scientists are exploring whether isolated β-bisabolene directly supports restful sleep. If true, it might explain some of the traditional uses of these botanicals.","evidence_level":"animal","paper_count":2,"study_to_test_it":"A first-in-human pilot trial giving participants a standardized β-bisabolene supplement or placebo before bed, measuring sleep latency and architecture via polysomnography over two weeks.","eco":{"id":"ECO:0007120","label":"animal model system study evidence"},"oxford":{"level":"5","label":"bench or animal research"},"evidence_lines":[{"design":"animal","eco":"ECO:0007120","papers":2}]}],"associated_conditions":[],"cautions":null,"evidence_at_a_glance":"Best evidence so far is human observational work (Oxford 2b) regarding its antimicrobial and antioxidant properties; most of the rest is animal and lab research."}}
